WEBVTT

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:03.125
Choosing a baseline is a risk and scope decision.

00:00:03.125 --> 00:00:04.360
Start with practical questions.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.545
What federal data will the service process, store, or transmit?

00:00:07.545 --> 00:00:11.480
What would happen if confidentiality, integrity, or availability were affected?

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.400
Which agencies or contracts are expected to use the service?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.475
What components, dependencies, customer responsibilities,

00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:19.140
and inherited services are in scope?

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.415
Which features are essential to the federal use case?

00:00:21.415 --> 00:00:25.715
Engineering teams support the baseline by building controls into the service rather than

00:00:25.715 --> 00:00:27.590
treating compliance as a separate overlay.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:32.120
That includes identity design, tenant boundaries, secure configuration, audit logging,

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:36.030
encryption decisions, vulnerability workflows, backup and recovery practices,

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.045
change management, and monitoring hooks.

00:00:38.045 --> 00:00:41.965
Security teams support the baseline by managing evidence, testing, risk tracking,

00:00:41.965 --> 00:00:44.245
incident response, and continuous monitoring.

00:00:44.245 --> 00:00:47.670
Product teams support it by avoiding commitments the service cannot sustain.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.320
Baseline work should involve the right stakeholders.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.310
Technical, security, compliance, legal, customer, operations,

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.880
and business leaders may all have relevant information.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.930
Legal and procurement decisions are outside the purpose of this course,

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:02.765
but the security team still needs to understand commitments, customer expectations,

00:01:02.765 --> 00:01:04.080
and scope boundaries.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.220
A baseline chosen in isolation can create mismatches between what the provider promises,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.410
what the system does, and what the agency needs.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.680
The most useful baseline discussions are honest and specific.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.000
If a control is inherited,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.020
show where it is inherited from and what remains for the service to do.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.945
If the customer has a responsibility, document it clearly.

00:01:20.945 --> 00:01:24.155
If a feature changes the boundary, treat that as a real scope issue.

00:01:24.155 --> 00:01:28.360
Baselines work best when teams use them to make risk visible, assign responsibility,

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.320
and keep security operations aligned with the service's federal use case.
